{"results":[{"url":"https://www.facebook.com/LWVTexas/posts/myth-if-youre-not-on-the-voter-roll-you-cant-vote-fact-if-you-believe-youre-regi/1551903730306220/","final_url":"https://www.facebook.com/LWVTexas/videos/myth-if-youre-not-on-the-voter-roll-you-cant-votefact-if-you-believe-youre-regis/2540272709792455/","title":"1.6K views · 25 reactions | MYTH: If you’re not on the voter roll, you can’t vote.\nFACT: If you believe you’re registered and eligible, you can cast a provisional ballot while the county confirms your eligibility. \n\nWhen is a provisional ballot the right call?\n\n✅ Use one if:\n▪️ You believe you’re registered, but poll workers can’t find you\n▪️ You don’t have an acceptable ID or alternative\n▪️ You canceled your mail ballot but didn’t turn it in\n\n❌  Don’t use one if:\n▪️ The machines are down or the polling place is out of ballots\n▪️ You’re at the wrong precinct in a county that requires you to vote at your own precinct. Go to the correct location instead. \n\nHere’s what to expect:\n▪️ Sign an affidavit envelope confirming you’re eligible, haven’t already voted, and live where you say you do.\n▪️ Cast your ballot following the poll workers’ instructions.\n▪️ Get a form explaining your next steps, and keep it.\n▪️ Within 30 days, you’ll get a notice in the mail telling you whether your vote counted. \n\nFollow through so your vote counts:\n▪️ ID issue? Bring an acceptable photo ID to your county elections office, or fill out a Reasonable Impediment Declaration there, by Nov. 9th.\n▪️ Registration questions? Contact your county right away with any proof of registration.\n▪️ Turned away? Call 866-OUR-VOTE from the parking lot before you leave.\n\nNo voter should be turned away without being offered a provisional ballot! | League of Women Voters of Texas","description":"MYTH: If you’re not on the voter roll, you can’t vote.\nFACT: If you believe you’re registered and eligible, you can cast a provisional ballot while the county confirms your eligibility. \n\nWhen is a...","language":"en","text":"MYTH: If you’re not on the voter roll, you can’t vote.\nFACT: If you believe you’re registered and eligible, you can cast a provisional ballot while the county confirms your eligibility.\nWhen is a provisional ballot the right call?\n✅ Use one if:\n▪️ You believe you’re registered, but poll workers can’t find you\n▪️ You don’t have an acceptable ID or alternative\n▪️ You canceled your mail ballot but didn’t turn it in\n❌ Don’t use one if:\n▪️ The machines are down or the polling place is out of ballots\n▪️ You’re at the wrong precinct in a county that requires you to vote at your own precinct. Go to the correct location instead.\nHere’s what to expect:\n▪️ Sign an affidavit envelope confirming you’re eligible, haven’t already voted, and live where you say you do.\n▪️ Cast your ballot following the poll workers’ instructions.\n▪️ Get a form explaining your next steps, and keep it.\n▪️ Within 30 days, you’ll get a notice in the mail telling you whether your vote counted.\nFollow through so your vote counts:\n▪️ ID issue? Bring an acceptable photo ID to your county elections office, or fill out a Reasonable Impediment Declaration there, by Nov. 9th.\n▪️ Registration questions? Contact your county right away with any proof of registration.\n▪️ Turned away? Call 866-OUR-VOTE from the parking lot before you leave.\nNo voter should be turned away without being offered a provisional ballot!","author":null,"published_date":null,"latency_ms":3709.496463998221,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}